   "text": {
    "L1": "Division asks how many groups of the divisor fit into the amount.",
    "L2": "This myth is wrong: dividing by a fraction less than one can make the answer bigger.",
    "L3": "For example, 12 quarters fit into 3, so 3 divided by one quarter is 12.",
    "L4": "The reciprocal of a fraction swaps its numerator and denominator.",
    "L5": "To divide by a fraction, keep the first fraction and flip only the divisor.",
    "L6": "Then multiply: two-thirds divided by four-fifths becomes two-thirds times five-fourths, which is five-sixths.",
    "L7": "In the worked example, three-fourths divided by three-eighths becomes three-fourths times eight-thirds, which simplifies to 2.",
    "L8": "Check by multiplying the answer by the divisor: 2 times three-eighths equals three-fourths.",
    "L9": "Now order the steps for dividing fractions."
   },
